pstore discards the whole buffer when the write cursor is ahead of the size,
even though the data is intact. On the surya that happens ALWAYS, and that is
why /sys/fs/pstore has come out empty in every session and there has never been
a trace of the boot hangs.

MEASURED LIKE THIS (2026-08-15): 200 counted lines were written, exactly 5490
bytes, into /dev/pmsg0. There was a hang, a hard button press and a boot. The
next boot said:

    ramoops: found existing invalid buffer, size 5234, start 5490

start is EXACTLY the 5490 bytes written: the data survived the forced power-off
in full. The only thing wrong is the size: 5234 (0x1472) against 5490 (0x1572).
They differ by ONE BIT.

And it explains the other symptom, which looked unrelated:

    pstore: zlib_inflate() failed, ret = -3!

The dmesg record is read truncated to "size", the compressed stream ends up cut
in half and fails to inflate. A single cause for both.

Why it happens: persistent_ram_write() advances "start" BEFORE writing and
"size" AFTER. Between the two there is a window, and if the device dies there
-or the header loses a bit on reset, which is what is seen here-, start > size
is left behind. The validity check treats that as garbage and calls
persistent_ram_zap().

This patch distinguishes the two cases that today are confused:

  - a counter that DOES NOT FIT in the zone: real garbage, thrown away as
    before.
  - start ahead of size, both within the zone: the header is half done but the
    data is there. It is trimmed and saved instead of thrown away.

Worst case: a few extra bytes are read at the end of the trace. In exchange for
having a trace, which is what there is not.

--- a/fs/pstore/ram_core.c
+++ b/fs/pstore/ram_core.c
@@ -543,11 +543,23 @@
 			return 0;
 		}

-		if (buffer_size(prz) > prz->buffer_size ||
-		    buffer_start(prz) > buffer_size(prz)) {
+		if (buffer_size(prz) > prz->buffer_size ||
+		    buffer_start(prz) > prz->buffer_size) {
+			/* A counter that does not fit in the region: garbage. */
 			pr_info("found existing invalid buffer, size %zu, start %zu\n",
 				buffer_size(prz), buffer_start(prz));
 			zap = true;
+		} else if (buffer_start(prz) > buffer_size(prz)) {
+			/*
+			 * The write cursor is ahead of the size: the
+			 * header was left half written, but the data up to "start"
+			 * is written. Before, the whole buffer was thrown away over
+			 * this, and with it the only trace of the failure.
+			 */
+			pr_info("found half-written buffer, size %zu, start %zu: recovering\n",
+				buffer_size(prz), buffer_start(prz));
+			atomic_set(&prz->buffer->size, buffer_start(prz));
+			persistent_ram_save_old(prz);
 		} else {
 			pr_debug("found existing buffer, size %zu, start %zu\n",
 				 buffer_size(prz), buffer_start(prz));
